1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,988
Click the plus after your trigger, search "AI Agent," and add it to your canvas.

2
00:00:04,988 --> 00:00:09,741
You will see a box with three empty sockets underneath it: Chat Model, Memory, and

3
00:00:09,741 --> 00:00:10,031
Tool.

4
00:00:10,031 --> 00:00:12,002
For this agent, you only need one.

5
00:00:12,002 --> 00:00:15,829
Attach a model, click the search icon, and pick one from the list.

6
00:00:15,829 --> 00:00:20,408
Why an AI Agent node, when the panel also offers plainer boxes that just call a

7
00:00:20,408 --> 00:00:20,756
model?

8
00:00:20,756 --> 00:00:25,220
Because the agent node is the one built to hold a standing instruction, and a

9
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:28,177
standing instruction is what you are here to write.

10
00:00:28,177 --> 00:00:31,133
The plain boxes send text and hand back text, once.

11
00:00:31,133 --> 00:00:35,655
The agent node holds who it is, judges whatever arrives, and grows sockets for

12
00:00:35,655 --> 00:00:38,554
memory and tools the day a later build needs them.

13
00:00:38,554 --> 00:00:40,873
You will use those sockets in Section 6.

14
00:00:40,873 --> 00:00:45,626
Today two of the three stay deliberately empty, and an empty socket on this canvas

15
00:00:45,626 --> 00:00:50,322
is the same kind of promise as a missing send node: visible, checkable restraint.

16
00:00:50,872 --> 00:00:55,566
Click Google Gemini Chat Model, then Set up credential, and paste in your API key

17
00:00:55,566 --> 00:00:56,435
from Section 2.

18
00:00:56,435 --> 00:00:58,000
That connects the thinking.

19
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,694
Pick the small, fast tier of the family, exactly as Section 2 argued: triage is a

20
00:01:02,694 --> 00:01:05,765
sorting job, and sorting belongs to the quick models.

21
00:01:05,765 --> 00:01:10,517
Once the credential saves, the box goes quiet, and the quiet is worth naming so it

22
00:01:10,517 --> 00:01:15,153
does not unsettle you: nothing has been sent anywhere yet. n8n only talks to the

23
00:01:15,153 --> 00:01:19,789
model when a run happens, so connecting the key costs nothing and risks nothing.

24
00:01:19,789 --> 00:01:24,599
The agent still does not know what you want it to think about, and that is the step

25
00:01:24,599 --> 00:01:25,874
you are about to take.

26
00:01:26,424 --> 00:01:28,854
You might expect this part to be complicated.

27
00:01:28,854 --> 00:01:33,174
It is really just you writing down, in plain sentences, the same judgement Priya

28
00:01:33,174 --> 00:01:35,334
already makes in her head every morning.

29
00:01:35,334 --> 00:01:39,599
The rule you are following: don't write clever instructions, write honest ones.

30
00:01:39,599 --> 00:01:44,135
One small habit as you write into these fields: draft the briefing somewhere you can

31
00:01:44,135 --> 00:01:47,915
think, a note or a document, and paste it in when it reads well aloud.

32
00:01:47,915 --> 00:01:52,451
The text box does not care, but briefings written directly into small boxes come out

33
00:01:52,451 --> 00:01:53,855
sounding like small boxes.

34
00:01:53,855 --> 00:01:56,771
If the split feels abstract, borrow the human version.

35
00:01:56,771 --> 00:02:01,306
When a new assistant starts, you brief them once: who we are, what matters here, how

36
00:02:01,306 --> 00:02:02,710
I want questions answered.

37
00:02:02,710 --> 00:02:04,654
That briefing is the System Message.

38
00:02:04,654 --> 00:02:09,136
Then every morning the post arrives, different every day, and they judge it against

39
00:02:09,136 --> 00:02:10,324
the standing briefing.

40
00:02:10,324 --> 00:02:14,752
The post is the User Message. n8n keeps the two in separate fields for a practical

41
00:02:14,752 --> 00:02:19,180
reason you will appreciate for months: when the agent's judgement needs adjusting,

42
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:23,554
and it will, you edit one paragraph in one place, without touching a single wire.

43
00:02:23,554 --> 00:02:27,873
Tuning an agent becomes rewriting a briefing, which is a skill you already have.

44
00:02:28,423 --> 00:02:30,043
Notice what you did not write.

45
00:02:30,043 --> 00:02:34,308
No code, no special syntax, nothing an engineer would recognise as a technique.

46
00:02:34,308 --> 00:02:38,788
Just a role, a decision, a format for the answer, and one instruction about what to

47
00:02:38,788 --> 00:02:39,166
ignore.

48
00:02:39,166 --> 00:02:43,431
That last sentence is doing more work for you than anything else in the prompt.

49
00:02:43,431 --> 00:02:47,966
Leave it out, and you will find your model flags everything that merely mentions the

50
00:02:47,966 --> 00:02:48,775
word insurance.

51
00:02:49,325 --> 00:02:53,564
Walk the paragraph with this table and the design stops looking like luck.

52
00:02:53,564 --> 00:02:58,262
The role line quietly settles judgement calls you would otherwise spell out one by

53
00:02:58,262 --> 00:02:58,491
one.

54
00:02:58,491 --> 00:03:03,188
The middle sentence should look familiar, because it is the "urgent means" line of

55
00:03:03,188 --> 00:03:07,771
the job description, nearly word for word: the document became the briefing, and

56
00:03:07,771 --> 00:03:09,317
that is the whole transfer.

57
00:03:09,317 --> 00:03:13,900
The reply format line is engineering you did without noticing, since routing can

58
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:17,394
only branch on an answer that arrives in a predictable shape.

59
00:03:17,394 --> 00:03:21,347
And the conservative line prices a trade this course refuses to hide.

60
00:03:21,347 --> 00:03:26,044
This prompt will occasionally skip a borderline email, and that is chosen, because

61
00:03:26,044 --> 00:03:30,856
the alternative is a flag list so noisy nobody reads it, and an ignored flag list is

62
00:03:30,856 --> 00:03:33,892
precisely how Ravi's message got lost the first time.

63
00:03:33,892 --> 00:03:35,439
A rare flag gets respected.

64
00:03:35,439 --> 00:03:37,100
That is what the flag is for.

65
00:03:37,650 --> 00:03:42,280
The mechanics: in the Prompt field, you build the message from the trigger's output

66
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:46,910
using the panel on the left, where every column of that wire table you fetched last

67
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:49,309
lecture is sitting, ready to be dragged in.

68
00:03:49,309 --> 00:03:53,883
Drop in the sender, the subject and the body, with a word of labelling around each

69
00:03:53,883 --> 00:03:55,780
so the model knows which is which.

70
00:03:55,780 --> 00:03:59,629
What you must not do is type an example email into the field by hand.

71
00:03:59,629 --> 00:04:03,925
It will work, beautifully, and it will keep judging that same imaginary email

72
00:04:03,925 --> 00:04:08,555
forever while real mail streams past it: the pinned data trap from Section 2 in its

73
00:04:08,555 --> 00:04:09,949
most convincing disguise.

74
00:04:09,949 --> 00:04:11,009
Point, never paste.

75
00:04:11,559 --> 00:04:14,343
Before you leave this lecture, spend one real call.

76
00:04:14,343 --> 00:04:18,765
Fetch a test email through the trigger, run the agent once, and read its one line

77
00:04:18,765 --> 00:04:20,785
answer out loud, the way Priya would.

78
00:04:20,785 --> 00:04:22,477
URGENT, with a reason, or SKIP.

79
00:04:22,477 --> 00:04:27,062
If the reason is sensible, you have just watched your own judgement come back to you

80
00:04:27,062 --> 00:04:31,593
from inside a workflow, and that moment is worth having before the routing goes in.

81
00:04:31,593 --> 00:04:36,015
If the reason is daft, even better: you have met your first tuning loop a lecture

82
00:04:36,015 --> 00:04:40,327
early, and the fix is the same one it will always be, a clearer sentence in the

83
00:04:40,327 --> 00:04:40,818
briefing.

84
00:04:40,818 --> 00:04:44,203
Either way the agent cannot yet do anything with its decision.

85
00:04:44,203 --> 00:04:46,113
That is your next lecture: routing.
